data.content:
Here's the approach — use .NET's built-in **`TimeProvider`** with an optional constructor parameter:

```csharp
public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
```

**Production** — callers don't change (`new InvoiceCalculator()` defaults to real time).

**Tests** — pass a `FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
// NuGet: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 5, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 5, 30, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 5, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is lightest:** no DI container, no custom interfaces, no ambient statics. `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+. For .NET 6/7, add the `Microsoft.Bcl.TimeProvider` NuGet package. Apply the same pattern to your other classes that read `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`.
